WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.297
INSERT adds new rows to a table.

00:00:02.297 --> 00:00:05.992
A good practice is to name the target columns explicitly.

00:00:05.992 --> 00:00:13.867
That makes it clear which value belongs to which field and makes the statement more resilient if the

00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:14.422
table structure later changes.

00:00:14.422 --> 00:00:20.532
PostgreSQL RETURNING can immediately show the row that was stored, including a generated primary key.

00:00:20.532 --> 00:00:26.277
Before running this example, look at the values and make sure the medical record number is unique and

00:00:26.277 --> 00:00:29.237
the age satisfies the table constraint.

00:00:29.237 --> 00:00:32.152
Then run it and inspect the returned row.

00:00:32.152 --> 00:00:39.042
If you copy the statement into pgAdmin, compare the returned values with a SELECT for the same unique

00:00:39.042 --> 00:00:46.357
key. INSERT changes stored data, so verification is now part of the workflow, not an optional extra step.

00:00:46.357 --> 00:00:49.757
Writes deserve a slower workflow than reads.

00:00:49.757 --> 00:00:56.302
Before UPDATE or DELETE, run the intended WHERE condition as a SELECT and inspect the affected rows.

00:00:56.302 --> 00:01:02.472
In production, use transactions when appropriate so you can verify the change before committing.

00:01:02.472 --> 00:01:07.120
The absence of a syntax error does not mean the correct records were modified.
